 think all of this  will be worked out so in terms of drafts  as I know certainly first hand in the  NFL having covered 15 of those yeah  there's always tons of parsing you can  you can pick apart anything and with  faults there's free-throw shooting and  then there's also the fact that like Ben  Simmons he did not lead his team to an  NCAA Tournament berth what what are the  concerns on those fronts for the Sixers  organization best you can hear yeah I  would raise a good point and that is we  could take any player at the top of the  draft and as we you know really dissect  the player his pluses minuses upside  what's the downside there's no perfect  there's no perfect pick so so much of it  pertains to a team's personal opinion on  a player and needs that a team may have  and so you know the one thing I'm very  certain of is that in the last year much  has come together for Philadelphia after  some really painful years of losing big  time and so you know I think we're on  the on the verge of really turning the  franchise around last year was a step  and indeed showed that he could be a  very very special player if he stays  healthy and you know we're assuming  Simmons is going to be good Simmons we  expect and you know I like the pieces  and I think there's a very bright future  for the 76ers Jerry Colangelo special  advisor to the Sixers as also the  managing director of USA Basketball  joining me here on the Rich Eisen show  when know when when do you think the  Sixers can actually put all these young  pieces together and start winning  basketball games and challenging teams  like the Celtics LeBron's Cavs for as  long as they're constituted that way  Jerry well you know you  mentioned earlier you know Paul George  and you know the free agent market and  how things are are changing this is a  different kind of a league at how its  operated in movement player movement etc  than it was ten years ago that's for  certainly um I think what you hope to do  is put pieces together and give those  give that collective group an  opportunity to develop together and as  you look at the core of what  Philadelphia is putting together there's  a core that if they stay healthy it live  up the expectations will be one of those  teams that can challenge anyone so let's  see how it all plays out over the next  couple of years this is not a flip the  switch you know all of a sudden you're a  contender kind of a situation but boy  it's all moving in the right direction  how much is the fact that the Warriors  are a super team and the Cavs appear to  be one and certainly if they can add  George or whomever that it pays to just  build for the long haul and wait LeBron  if not the Warriors out Jerry well I  think you raised another good point and  that is it is what it is in the NBA and  there are super a couple of teams that  have separated themselves from the pack  everyone else is doing their job trying  to build trying to grow trying to  compete and you know all things come to  an end as you well know you know teams  don't win forever players you know get  hurt players get old players move on and  free agency and so it's a it's a moving  target stay on course stick to your guns  develop your own players and I think  that's a key to this and then you may be  adding a free agent here or there and  then you're ready to truly compete is  this a good thing for the NBA  super team's journey I you know I think  there's pluses and minuses I don't have  a problem with it  first of all I like to give credit to  those who have become that kind of a  product  uh give them credit for doing that and  there's something to be said for the  great rivalries between great teams and  you know everyone else would like to be  one of them so when uh what do you think  if you wouldn't mind putting on your  decades of experience on this front  Jerry what do you think the Pacers  options are right now now that it got  out there that Paul George wants out and  even mentioned the Lakers is a specific  team what what what options you think  are available to the Pacers right now  well not a lot you know it's it's kind  of a bad situation to have your player  you know publicly state that you know  he's going to be gone and so you know  just it's it's a it's a difficult  dilemma for for the Pacers and it'll be  interesting to see how it does play out  well I mean if the do you think they  could pry loose say the Lakers second  overall pick for something like this  it's certainly if the Cavs are in the  mix or or the Celtics are in the mix  Gerry well free agency you know is if  you if you're willing to wait for free  agent and his timing you don't have to  worry about compensation you don't have  to worry about anything like that  conversely you know our team's going to  be interested in the player any player  who has indicated I'm only going to be  there for a cup of coffee because I want  to be somewhere else you know a year  from now that that is not an easy you  know there's no real easy answer here is  the point rich and know there's a report  on the worldwide leader in sports ESPN  that the Sixers turned down an offer  from the Bulls for the third overall  selection in the form of Jimmy Butler is  there any truth to that Gerry I have no  idea and the reason I say that rich is  that I am now in transit to Philadelphia  for the week okay for the draft and I'm  going to get caught up at all these  rumors a night when I get there  so I'm just trying to figure out what  you know because I  certainly know that Butler is extremely  talented and I'm just now going to go  hypothetical with you here what is more  valuable a bona fide all-star in a prime  of his career or a draft choice for  somebody who's 18 19 20 years old what  how is that viewed within the Ndege  community  well again you can't just isolate it it  depends on what else you have on your  roster and where your team developed in  is so it could fit very well for one  team 